\label{ch:target-machines}

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:target-intro}

The code generator's ultimate task is to translate an intermediate representation into a sequence of machine instructions that execute correctly and efficiently on a specific target architecture. Before any translation can occur, the compiler must possess a precise model of the target machine: its instruction set, register file, addressing modes, memory hierarchy, pipeline structure, and special-purpose functional units. This chapter develops a comprehensive theory of target machine modeling, providing the formal infrastructure upon which instruction selection, register allocation, and instruction scheduling---the subjects of subsequent chapters---are built.

A target machine model is not merely a list of instructions. It is a mathematical abstraction that captures those aspects of the hardware relevant to code generation while eliding those details that are the province of microarchitectural engineering. The art lies in choosing the right level of abstraction: too coarse, and the compiler cannot exploit important hardware features; too fine, and the model becomes unwieldy and the generated code overly sensitive to microarchitectural details that change between processor revisions.

We begin with instruction set architectures (ISAs), the programmer-visible contract between hardware and software. We then examine register files and addressing modes, the mechanisms by which instructions access operands. Pipeline models and hazard detection follow, capturing the temporal constraints that govern instruction throughput. The memory hierarchy---caches, TLBs, and main memory---introduces spatial and temporal considerations that profoundly affect performance. We conclude with SIMD and vector units, which expose data-level parallelism, and a discussion of how these models are integrated into compiler infrastructure.

\section{Instruction Set Architectures}\label{sec:isa}

\subsection{What Is an ISA?}\label{sec:isa-definition}

\begin{definition}[Instruction Set Architecture]\label{def:isa}
An \emph{instruction set architecture} (ISA) is a formal specification of the programmer-visible state of a processor and the instructions that manipulate that state. Formally, an ISA is a tuple
\[
  \mathcal{A} = (\mathcal{S}, \mathcal{I}, \mathcal{M}, \mathcal{E})
\]
where $\mathcal{S}$ is the set of architecturally visible states (register files, memory, status flags), $\mathcal{I}$ is the set of instructions, $\mathcal{M}$ is the memory model defining how instructions interact with the address space, and $\mathcal{E}$ is the exception and interrupt model.
\end{definition}

The ISA is the boundary between hardware and software. It is the contract that allows a compiler to generate code without knowledge of the specific microarchitectural implementation---the pipeline depth, the cache sizes, the branch prediction algorithm---while still guaranteeing correct execution. This separation of concerns is one of the great engineering achievements of computer science.

\subsection{ISA Classification}\label{sec:isa-classification}

Historically, ISAs have been classified along several axes. The most fundamental distinction is between \emph{load--store} (RISC) architectures and \emph{register--memory} (CISC) architectures.

\begin{definition}[Load--Store Architecture]\label{def:load-store}
A \emph{load--store architecture} is one in which arithmetic and logical operations can only access operands in registers; memory is accessed exclusively through explicit load and store instructions.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Register--Memory Architecture]\label{def:reg-mem}
A \emph{register--memory architecture} (also called a \emph{memory--memory architecture}) permits arithmetic and logical operations to reference operands directly in memory, using various addressing modes.
\end{definition}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Comparison of ISA design philosophies}
\label{tab:isa-comparison}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\hline
\textbf{Feature} & \textbf{RISC (Load--Store)} & \textbf{CISC (Reg--Mem)} \\
\hline
Instruction length & Fixed or limited & Variable \\
Addressing modes & Few, simple & Many, complex \\
Memory operations & Explicit load/store & Embedded in arithmetic \\
Pipeline friendliness & High & Moderate \\
Compiler complexity & Higher (more instructions) & Lower (complex instr.) \\
Microcode & Minimal & Extensive \\
Examples & ARM, RISC-V, MIPS & x86, VAX \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Modern ISAs blur this distinction. The x86-64 architecture, while historically CISC, has an internal RISC-like micro-operation decomposition. ARM, while historically RISC, has added instructions like \texttt{LDR} with auto-indexing that approach CISC complexity. The classification remains useful as a design philosophy, but the practical differences have narrowed considerably.

\subsection{Instruction Encoding}\label{sec:instruction-encoding}

Every instruction in an ISA has an \emph{encoding}: a binary representation that the instruction decoder interprets. The encoding determines the instruction's opcode, operand specifiers, and immediate values.

\begin{definition}[Instruction Format]\label{def:instr-format}
An \emph{instruction format} is a partitioning of the instruction's binary encoding into fields, each with a specific interpretation:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{Opcode field}: identifies the operation
  \item \emph{Register specifier fields}: identify source and destination registers
  \item \emph{Immediate field}: encodes a constant operand
  \item \emph{Address field}: encodes a memory address or displacement
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[RISC-V Instruction Formats]\label{ex:riscv-formats}
The RISC-V ISA defines several instruction formats, each 32 bits wide in the base ISA:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{R-type}: \texttt{[funct7(7)][rs2(5)][rs1(5)][funct3(3)][rd(5)][opcode(7)]}
  \item \textbf{I-type}: \texttt{[imm[11:0](12)][rs1(5)][funct3(3)][rd(5)][opcode(7)]}
  \item \textbf{S-type}: \texttt{[imm[11:5](7)][rs2(5)][rs1(5)][funct3(3)][imm[4:0](5)][opcode(7)]}
  \item \textbf{B-type}: \texttt{[imm[12|10:5](7)][rs2(5)][rs1(5)][funct3(3)][imm[4:1|11](5)][opcode(7)]}
\end{itemize}
The opcode field determines the format; the funct3 and funct7 fields further distinguish operations within a format.
\end{example}

For the compiler, instruction encoding matters primarily in two respects: (1)~code size, which affects instruction cache performance and memory bandwidth, and (2)~the constraints it imposes on instruction selection, since not all combinations of operands and operations may be directly encodable.

\subsection{Orthogonality and Regularity}\label{sec:orthogonality}

An ideal ISA is \emph{orthogonal}: any instruction can be combined with any addressing mode and any register. Real ISAs deviate from this ideal in various ways.

\begin{definition}[Orthogonality]\label{def:orthogonality}
An ISA is \emph{orthogonal} if the set of available addressing modes is independent of the instruction being encoded, and if all general-purpose registers are interchangeable.
\end{definition}

Deviations from orthogonality are common and important for code generation:
\begin{itemize}
  \item x86-64 restricts certain shift and rotate instructions to use \texttt{CL} as the shift count register.
  \item ARM's legacy 32-bit ISA has register \texttt{r15} (PC) that cannot always be used as a general-purpose register.
  \item x86's \texttt{MUL}/\texttt{DIV} instructions implicitly use the \texttt{RAX}/\texttt{RDX} register pair.
  \item Many architectures restrict the set of registers usable as base registers in certain addressing modes.
\end{itemize}

The compiler must model these irregularities precisely. Failure to do so leads to illegal instruction sequences or missed optimization opportunities.

\section{Register Files and Addressing Modes}\label{sec:registers}

\subsection{Register File Organization}\label{sec:reg-file-org}

Modern processors expose multiple register files, each serving a distinct purpose:

\begin{definition}[Register File]\label{def:reg-file}
A \emph{register file} is a finite collection of named storage locations, each holding a value of fixed width, that are directly accessible by name in the instruction encoding.
\end{definition}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Register file organization in modern ISAs}
\label{tab:reg-files}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\hline
\textbf{Architecture} & \textbf{GPRs} & \textbf{FP/SIMD} & \textbf{Width} \\
\hline
x86-64 & 16 & 16 (XMM/YMM/ZMM) & 64 / 128--512 \\
ARM AArch64 & 31 + SP & 32 (V0--V31) & 64 / 128 \\
RISC-V RV64 & 32 & 32 (F/D/Q ext.) & 64 / 32--128 \\
MIPS64 & 32 & 32 & 64 / 64 \\
Power ISA 3.0 & 32 & 32 + 64 VSX & 64 / 128 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The number and organization of registers profoundly affects code quality. More registers reduce the pressure on the register allocator (\cref{ch:register-allocation}), allowing more values to be kept in fast storage. However, more registers also increase the encoding size needed to specify them and increase context-switch overhead.

\subsection{Special-Purpose Registers}\label{sec:special-regs}

Beyond general-purpose registers, ISAs define various special-purpose registers:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Program counter (PC)}: holds the address of the next instruction to execute. May be implicit (x86) or explicit (ARM, RISC-V).
  \item \textbf{Stack pointer (SP)}: points to the top of the hardware stack. Some ISAs provide hardware stack push/pop; others require explicit arithmetic.
  \item \textbf{Frame pointer (FP)}: points to a fixed location in the current stack frame, enabling reliable stack traversal. Often omitted in optimized code.
  \item \textbf{Status/condition flags}: carry, zero, overflow, sign, and other flags set by arithmetic operations and tested by conditional branches.
  \item \textbf{Link register (LR)}: on architectures with branch-and-link instructions (ARM, PowerPC), stores the return address for subroutine calls.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Addressing Modes}\label{sec:addressing-modes}

An addressing mode specifies how the effective address of a memory operand is computed from the fields of an instruction.

\begin{definition}[Addressing Mode]\label{def:addr-mode}
An \emph{addressing mode} is a rule for computing the \emph{effective address} (EA) of an operand from the instruction's fields and the processor's register state.
\end{definition}

Common addressing modes include:

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Common addressing modes}
\label{tab:addr-modes}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
\textbf{Mode} & \textbf{Effective Address} & \textbf{Example} \\
\hline
Register & EA = $R_i$ & \texttt{MOV R1, R2} \\
Immediate & operand = $\mathit{imm}$ & \texttt{ADD R1, \#42} \\
Absolute & EA = $\mathit{addr}$ & \texttt{LOAD R1, [0x1000]} \\
Register indirect & EA = $R_i$ & \texttt{LOAD R1, [R2]} \\
Base + displacement & EA = $R_i + d$ & \texttt{LOAD R1, 8(R2)} \\
Base + index & EA = $R_i + R_j$ & \texttt{LOAD R1, [R2+R3]} \\
Base + index $\times$ scale & EA = $R_i + R_j \times s$ & \texttt{LOAD R1, [R2+R4*4]} \\
PC-relative & EA = $\text{PC} + d$ & \texttt{LOAD R1, \text{label}(PC)} \\
Pre-indexed & EA = $R_i + d$; $R_i \leftarrow \text{EA}$ & \texttt{LDR R1, [R2, \#8]!} \\
Post-indexed & EA = $R_i$; $R_i \leftarrow R_i + d$ & \texttt{LDR R1, [R2], \#8} \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{insight}
The choice of addressing modes is a critical design decision for ISA architects. Rich addressing modes reduce instruction count (and thus code size) but complicate the instruction decoder and pipeline. The modern trend favors a small set of addressing modes---register indirect with displacement, and base-plus-index with scale---that cover the vast majority of access patterns in compiled code.
\end{insight}

\subsection{Addressing Mode Selection}\label{sec:addr-mode-selection}

For the code generator, addressing mode selection is a key subproblem of instruction selection. Given a memory access in the IR, the compiler must choose which addressing mode to use, considering:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Availability}: not all modes are available for all instructions on all architectures.
  \item \item \textbf{Cost}: complex modes may take more cycles or require additional micro-operations.
  \item \textbf{Code size}: some modes encode more compactly than others.
  \item \textbf{Register pressure}: using a register as a base or index consumes that register.
  \item \textbf{Address computation reuse}: if the same address is computed multiple times, a single base register with varying displacements is preferable.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Pipeline Models and Hazards}\label{sec:pipeline}

\subsection{Pipeline Structure}\label{sec:pipeline-structure}

Modern processors execute instructions in a \emph{pipeline}: a sequence of stages through which each instruction passes. The classical five-stage RISC pipeline consists of:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Instruction Fetch (IF)}: retrieve the instruction from the instruction cache.
  \item \textbf{Instruction Decode (ID)}: decode the opcode and read register operands.
  \item \textbf{Execute (EX)}: perform the arithmetic or address computation.
  \item \textbf{Memory (MEM)}: access data memory (for loads and stores).
  \item \textbf{Write Back (WB)}: write the result to the register file.
\end{enumerate}

