\label{fig:five-stage-pipeline}
\end{figure}

While real pipelines are far more complex (modern superscalar processors may have 14+ stages with multiple parallel execution paths), the five-stage model captures the essential structure needed for the compiler to reason about hazards.

\subsection{Structural Hazards}\label{sec:structural-hazards}

A \emph{structural hazard} occurs when two instructions in the pipeline simultaneously require the same hardware resource.

\begin{definition}[Structural Hazard]\label{def:structural-hazard}
A \emph{structural hazard} arises at cycle $t$ when instructions $I_i$ and $I_j$, both active in the pipeline at cycle $t$, require access to the same hardware resource, and that resource cannot service both requests simultaneously.
\end{definition}

Common structural hazards include:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Memory port conflicts}: a unified instruction/data cache cannot serve both an instruction fetch and a data load in the same cycle.
  \item \textbf{Register file write conflicts}: multiple instructions completing in the same cycle may require simultaneous writes to the register file.
  \item \textbf{Functional unit conflicts}: two instructions requiring the same ALU or multiplier.
\end{itemize}

For the compiler, structural hazards are typically handled by the hardware (through resource duplication or stalling). However, the compiler can improve performance by scheduling instructions to avoid resource conflicts.

\subsection{Data Hazards}\label{sec:data-hazards}

A \emph{data hazard} occurs when an instruction depends on the result of a prior instruction that is still in the pipeline.

\begin{definition}[Data Hazard Types]\label{def:data-hazards}
Given two instructions $I_1$ (earlier) and $I_2$ (later) in program order:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Read After Write (RAW)}: $I_2$ reads a register/memory location that $I_1$ writes. This is a \emph{true dependency}.
  \item \textbf{Write After Read (WAR)}: $I_2$ writes a location that $I_1$ reads. This is an \emph{anti-dependency}.
  \item \textbf{Write After Write (WAW)}: $I_2$ writes the same location as $I_1$. This is an \emph{output dependency}.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Load-Use Hazard]\label{ex:load-use}
Consider the instruction sequence:
\begin{verbatim}
  LOAD  R1, 0(R2)     ; R1 <- Mem[R2]
  ADD   R3, R1, R4    ; R3 <- R1 + R4
\end{verbatim}
The \texttt{ADD} depends on the result of \texttt{LOAD}. In a five-stage pipeline, the \texttt{LOAD} produces its result at the end of the MEM stage, but the \texttt{ADD} needs the value at the beginning of the EX stage---one cycle earlier. This is a \emph{load-use hazard} requiring a one-cycle stall (or \emph{bubble}) unless the processor supports \emph{data forwarding} (bypassing).
\end{example}

\subsection{Control Hazards}\label{sec:control-hazards}

A \emph{control hazard} arises when the pipeline must fetch the next instruction but the target of a branch or jump is not yet known.

\begin{definition}[Control Hazard]\label{def:control-hazard}
A \emph{control hazard} occurs when a branch or jump instruction is in the pipeline and the fetch stage must decide which instruction to retrieve next, but the branch target or outcome has not yet been resolved.
\end{definition}

The penalty for a control hazard depends on the branch resolution latency and the effectiveness of branch prediction. Modern processors achieve prediction accuracies above 95\%, but the remaining mispredictions are a significant source of performance loss.

For the compiler, control hazards motivate several optimizations:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Branch delay slots}: on some architectures (MIPS, SPARC), the instruction after a branch is always executed. The compiler must fill this slot with a useful instruction.
  \item \textbf{Code layout}: placing the likely-taken branch target immediately after the branch reduces the prediction penalty.
  \item \textbf{Branch reduction}: transforming conditional branches into conditional moves or predicated instructions eliminates the hazard entirely.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Modeling Pipelines for the Compiler}\label{sec:pipeline-modeling}

The compiler does not need a cycle-accurate pipeline model. Instead, it needs an abstract model that captures the constraints relevant to instruction scheduling. This model typically consists of:

\begin{definition}[Pipeline Model for Scheduling]\label{def:pipeline-model}
A \emph{pipeline model} for instruction scheduling is a triple $(\mathcal{R}, \mathcal{L}, \mathcal{D})$ where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\mathcal{R}$ is a set of \emph{resources} (functional units, ports, buses) with capacities
  \item $\mathcal{L}: \mathcal{I} \to \mathbb{N}$ is a \emph{latency function} mapping each instruction to its execution latency
  \item $\mathcal{D}: \mathcal{I} \times \mathcal{I} \to \mathbb{N}$ is a \emph{delay function} mapping pairs of instructions to the minimum issue delay between them
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

This model is sufficient for list scheduling (\cref{ch:instruction-scheduling}) and captures the essential timing constraints without requiring a full microarchitectural simulation.

\section{Memory Hierarchy}\label{sec:memory-hierarchy}

\subsection{The Memory Hierarchy}\label{sec:mem-hierarchy-structure}

The memory system of a modern processor is organized as a hierarchy of storage levels, each trading capacity for speed:

