\caption{The memory hierarchy}
\label{fig:memory-hierarchy}
\end{figure}

The compiler's interaction with the memory hierarchy is primarily through the \emph{locality} of the generated code. Two forms of locality are relevant:

\begin{definition}[Locality]\label{def:locality}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{Temporal locality}: a memory location accessed at time $t$ is likely to be accessed again in the near future.
  \item \emph{Spatial locality}: a memory location accessed at time $t$ is likely to have nearby locations accessed in the near future.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Cache Organization}\label{sec:cache-org}

Caches are organized into \emph{lines} (or \emph{blocks}), typically 64 bytes on modern processors. Each line is the unit of transfer between cache levels.

\begin{definition}[Cache Parameters]\label{def:cache-params}
A cache is characterized by:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $C$: total cache capacity in bytes
  \item $B$: line (block) size in bytes
  \item $E$: associativity (number of lines per set)
  \item $S = C / (B \times E)$: number of sets
  \item A line with address $a$ maps to set $s = (a / B) \bmod S$
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

The compiler must be aware of cache behavior for several reasons:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Cache-friendly data layouts}: structure layout, array ordering (row-major vs. column-major), and padding affect cache utilization.
  \item \textbf{Loop transformations}: interchange, fusion, fission, and tiling (\cref{ch:loop-opt}) restructure access patterns to improve locality.
  \item \textbf{Prefetching}: explicit prefetch instructions can hide memory latency when access patterns are predictable.
  \item \textbf{Cache coloring}: partitioning the address space to reduce cache conflicts between critical code paths.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Virtual Memory and TLB}\label{sec:virtual-memory}

Modern systems use \emph{virtual memory}, where each process sees a large, contiguous address space that is mapped to physical memory by the hardware \emph{memory management unit} (MMU). The translation is cached in the \emph{translation lookaside buffer} (TLB).

\begin{definition}[TLB]\label{def:tlb}
The \emph{translation lookaside buffer} (TLB) is a hardware cache that stores recent virtual-to-physical address translations. A TLB miss requires a page table walk, which may take hundreds of cycles.
\end{definition}

For the compiler, TLB behavior matters when code accesses many distinct pages. Large arrays accessed with large strides can cause TLB thrashing. The compiler can mitigate this through:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Using \emph{huge pages} (2MB or 1GB) when available, reducing the number of TLB entries needed.
  \item Restructuring data layouts to reduce the working set's page footprint.
  \item Avoiding unnecessary pointer indirections that scatter accesses across memory.
\end{itemize}

\section{SIMD and Vector Units}\label{sec:simd}

\subsection{Data-Level Parallelism}\label{sec:data-parallelism}

\emph{Single Instruction, Multiple Data} (SIMD) architectures exploit data-level parallelism by applying the same operation to multiple data elements simultaneously.

\begin{definition}[SIMD]\label{def:simd}
A \emph{SIMD} (Single Instruction, Multiple Data) instruction performs the same operation on $w$ data elements packed into a wide register, where $w$ is the \emph{vector width}. For example, a 256-bit SIMD register can hold eight 32-bit integers, and a single SIMD \texttt{ADD} instruction adds all eight pairs simultaneously.
\end{definition}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{SIMD extensions across architectures}
\label{tab:simd-extensions}
\begin{tabular}{llcc}
\hline
\textbf{Architecture} & \textbf{Extension} & \textbf{Max Width} & \textbf{Elements (32-bit)} \\
\hline
x86 & SSE & 128-bit & 4 \\
x86 & AVX2 & 256-bit & 8 \\
x86 & AVX-512 & 512-bit & 16 \\
ARM & NEON & 128-bit & 4 \\
ARM & SVE & 128--2048-bit & variable \\
RISC-V & RVV 1.0 & variable & variable \\
Power & VSX/VMX & 128-bit & 4 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Vectorization}\label{sec:vectorization}

\emph{Vectorization} is the compiler transformation that converts scalar loops into SIMD instructions. The key challenge is determining when vectorization is legal and beneficial.

\begin{definition}[Vectorization Condition]\label{def:vectorization}
A loop is \emph{vectorizable} if:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item The loop has a known trip count (or can be handled with masking for the tail).
  \item There are no loop-carried dependencies that prevent parallel execution of iterations.
  \item Memory accesses are contiguous (or can be gathered/scattered).
  \item The operations in the loop body have corresponding SIMD instructions.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Vectorizable Loop]\label{ex:vectorizable}
The following loop is vectorizable because each iteration is independent:
\begin{verbatim}
  for (int i = 0; i < N; i++)
    C[i] = A[i] + B[i];
\end{verbatim}
A SIMD implementation processes 8 elements at a time (with AVX2):
\begin{verbatim}
  for (int i = 0; i < N; i += 8)
    _mm256_store_ps(&C[i],
      _mm256_add_ps(_mm256_load_ps(&A[i]),
                     _mm256_load_ps(&B[i])));
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Vector Length Agnosticism}\label{sec:vla}

A modern trend in SIMD design is \emph{vector length agnosticism} (VLA), where the compiler generates code that works correctly regardless of the hardware vector width. ARM's SVE and RISC-V's RVV both support this approach.

\begin{definition}[Vector Length Agnostic Code]\label{def:vla}
\emph{Vector length agnostic} (VLA) code is written in terms of an abstract vector length that is determined at runtime. The same binary executes correctly on hardware with different vector widths.
\end{definition}

VLA code typically uses \emph{predication} or \emph{masking} to handle the tail of a loop when the trip count is not a multiple of the vector width. This eliminates the need for multiple code versions for different vector widths.

\section{Modeling Targets for Code Generation}\label{sec:target-modeling}

\subsection{Machine Descriptions}\label{sec:machine-descriptions}

A compiler's \emph{machine description} (or \emph{target description}) is a data structure that encodes all the information the compiler needs about the target machine. It typically includes:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Register definitions}: names, classes, sizes, and costs of all registers.
  \item \textbf{Instruction definitions}: opcodes, formats, latencies, resource usage, and semantics.
  \item \textbf{Addressing mode definitions}: available modes and their costs.
  \item \textbf{Calling convention}: argument passing, return values, callee-saved registers.
  \item \textbf{Cost model}: relative costs of operations for instruction selection.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{TableGen and Machine Descriptions}\label{sec:tablegen}

LLVM's \emph{TableGen} system provides a domain-specific language for describing target machines. A TableGen description includes:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{Register} definitions with register classes and sub-registers
  \item \texttt{Instruction} definitions with patterns mapping IR operations to machine instructions
  \item \texttt{Predicate} definitions for conditional instruction availability
  \item \texttt{Scheduling} models for instruction latencies and resource usage
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}[TableGen Register Definition]\label{ex:tablegen-reg}
A simplified TableGen description of x86-64 general-purpose registers:
\begin{verbatim}
  def GR64 : RegisterClass<"X86", [i64], 64,
    (add RAX, RCX, RDX, RBX, RSP, RBP, RSI, RDI,
         R8,  R9,  R10, R11, R12, R13, R14, R15)>;
\end{verbatim}
This defines a register class \texttt{GR64} containing 16 64-bit registers, each holding an \texttt{i64} value.
\end{example}

\subsection{Constraint Solving for Target Modeling}\label{sec:constraint-solving}

Some target constraints are naturally expressed as a constraint satisfaction problem. For example, instruction selection with addressing mode constraints can be modeled as:

\begin{definition}[Instruction Selection as CSP]\label{def:is-csp}
Given an IR expression tree $T$, instruction selection is the problem of finding a covering of $T$ by instruction patterns such that:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Every node in $T$ is covered by exactly one pattern.
  \item The patterns used are valid for the target (respecting register constraints, addressing mode restrictions, etc.).
  \item The total cost (sum of pattern costs) is minimized.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

This formulation connects instruction selection to well-studied problems in combinatorial optimization, enabling the use of integer linear programming or constraint programming techniques for optimal code generation.

\subsection{Retargetable Compilation}\label{sec:retargetable}

A \emph{retargetable} compiler can generate code for multiple target architectures from a single compiler codebase. The key to retargetability is the separation of the machine-independent and machine-dependent components:

