\subsection{Estimating Throughput with Flexible Execution Ports}

Counting operations against a single nominal port per instruction can give the wrong bottleneck. Some micro-operations have several legal ports, while others are restricted to one. A useful target description records those alternatives. A matching test can then answer whether a given throughput is even possible before considering latencies or the order of issue.

\textbf{Inputs and assumptions.} Expand one iteration of an independent, repeating instruction mix into unit-demand micro-operations. Each has a known set of legal ports, with one operation per port per cycle. We test a period of $T$ whole cycles per iteration. This intentionally ignores front-end limits, data dependencies, and multi-cycle reservations; passing the test is not a prediction of achieved throughput.

\begin{sudocode}[Test a port-capacity bound]
\Require Micro-operations $U$, ports $P$, period $T$
\Ensure Port assignment within the period or infeasibility
\State Create $T$ unit-capacity slots for each port
\ForAll{$u\in U$}
  \ForAll{ports $p$ eligible for $u$}
    \State Connect $u$ to each slot of $p$
  \EndFor
\EndFor
\State $M\gets$ maximum bipartite matching
\State \Comment{An augmenting path may move a flexible operation}
\If{$|M|<|U|$}
  \State \Return infeasible \Comment{A capacity limit, not a heuristic}
\EndIf
\State \Return assignments in $M$
\end{sudocode}

\textbf{Example.} Ports $P_0,P_1$ can each take one operation per cycle. Two adds can use either port; two shifts can use only $P_0$. For $T=1$ there are two slots for four operations, so the test fails. At $T=2$ it succeeds by assigning both shifts to $P_0$ and both adds to $P_1$. With a third shift, even $T=2$ fails despite the presence of free slots on $P_1$.

\Cref{fig:visual-83} shows that the total number of available slots
does not tell the whole story: an idle slot helps only if the
unmatched operation is eligible to use it.

