\textbf{Invariant.} Every matched operation occupies just one legal slot, and no slot serves two operations. A capacity-flow formulation avoids explicitly duplicating slots for large $T$. Feasibility is monotone in $T$, so a compiler can binary-search the smallest feasible whole-cycle period. A dependency chain can still enforce more cycles than any port calculation predicts.

\section{Summary and Outlook}\label{sec:target-summary}

The target machine model is the foundation upon which all back-end compiler phases are built. A precise, well-structured model enables effective instruction selection, register allocation, and scheduling. An imprecise model leads to incorrect or suboptimal code.

The trends in modern processor design---increasing pipeline depth, out-of-order execution, heterogeneous cores, and specialized accelerators---continually challenge the compiler's target model. The model must be rich enough to capture the features that matter for performance while remaining abstract enough to be portable across microarchitectural generations.

In the chapters that follow, we build on this foundation. \Cref{ch:code-emission} addresses the production of object files and executables. \Cref{ch:calling-conventions} formalizes the interface between compiled code units. \Cref{ch:instruction-selection,ch:register-allocation,ch:instruction-scheduling} develop the core back-end phases that transform IR into efficient machine code.

\begin{historical}
The study of target machine modeling for compilers began with the early work on machine-independent code generation by Cocke and others at IBM in the 1970s. The idea of using tree pattern matching for instruction selection, developed by Aho, Johnson, and others, was a breakthrough that made retargetable compilation practical. The TableGen approach, pioneered by LLVM in the early 2000s, further advanced retargetability by providing a declarative language for machine descriptions. The increasing complexity of modern ISAs---with SIMD extensions, heterogeneous cores, and accelerator interfaces---continues to push the boundaries of target modeling.
\end{historical}
